  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1019 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nOver the next few days, a split flow pattern develops, with the\nclosed low over the SW CONUS moving slower than a low in the polar\njet stream over Canada. Pressure falls in the lee of the northern\nRockies will lead to a few breezy days locally, with southerly \ngusts generally between 25-35 mph each day Fri-Sun. \n\nThe gradual approach of the upper low will also bring increasing\nprecip chances over the weekend. Guidance continues to trend \nslower with the arrival of precip chances, now keeping precip \nchances west of I-27 on Saturday-Saturday evening, eventually \nspreading east overnight into Sun. Precip chances then persist \narea-wide Sun-Tues. Once again, precipitable water (PWAT) values \nare set to be unseasonably high, around 1.25\" which is above the \n90th percentile. This kind of moisture content can support locally\nheavy rainfall and flooding. For now, ensemble guidance has the \nhighest chance of exceeding 2\" of rainfall through Tuesday morning\nfocused across the far SW TX Panhandle (40-50%), with a 20-40% \nchance across the rest of the Caprock and just a low chance (less \nthan 20%) off the Caprock. In terms of severe storm potential, \ninstability looks like a limiting factor as meager lapse rates \nmake it struggle to achieve more than 500 J/kg of CAPE. Still, the\nhigh PWAT values could support sporadic downbursts, and roughly \n40 knots of deep layer shear suggest the kinematics will be \nsupportive should the instability progs improve. Thus, we can't \ncompletely write off the severe potential just yet, even if it \nappears low at this time.\n\nTemps gradually trend cooler as this system shifts into the area,\nwith below normal temps forecast area-wide Sun-Tues (low 60s to \nlow 70s). There should be plenty of cloud cover to help keep \novernight lows from dropping too cold, generally in the 50s or \nupper 40s, so frost/freeze is not a concern.",
